  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R3-031$qb$, $qb$A5-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague owns a delegated task and asks you to approve a small decision that is within the agreed limits. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Remind them that the decision is within their authority and let them decide.$qb$, $qb$Confirm they can decide and ask them to keep you informed.$qb$, $qb$Approve it this time so the work keeps moving.$qb$, $qb$Take back decisions that could affect the outcome.$qb$, $qb$Require your approval for all future decisions.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R5-031$qb$, $qb$A5-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A delegated task is on track, but the colleague chooses a different sequence from yours. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Focus on the agreed result and intervene only if the different sequence creates a real risk$qb$, $qb$Ask them to explain the sequence and continue if it meets the requirements$qb$, $qb$Tell them to follow your sequence because you know the task$qb$, $qb$Review every step before they proceed$qb$, $qb$Take the task back$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R6-031$qb$, $qb$A5-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You delegate a task and the colleague identifies a small risk you had not considered. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Discuss the risk, adjust the boundaries if needed, and let them continue ownership$qb$, $qb$Thank them for raising it and agree how to manage it$qb$, $qb$Take the task back because the risk was unexpected$qb$, $qb$Tell them to follow the original plan anyway$qb$, $qb$Ignore the risk because it is small$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R8-031$qb$, $qb$A5-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague completes a delegated task successfully without using the method you expected. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Recognize the result and review the different method only for lessons or risks$qb$, $qb$Ask them to explain their method and what worked$qb$, $qb$Tell them to use your method next time$qb$, $qb$Redo the task using your method$qb$, $qb$Reduce their freedom on future tasks$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R9-031$qb$, $qb$A5-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague asks permission for a routine choice that is clearly within the authority you gave them. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm that they can make the choice and reinforce the agreed decision limits$qb$, $qb$Remind them of their authority and ask them to proceed$qb$, $qb$Approve the choice this time$qb$, $qb$Take over the decision because they asked$qb$, $qb$Require approval for all future routine choices$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-REF-031$qb$, $qb$A5-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$How can a project coordinator effectively empower team members when delegating operational tasks?$qb$, $qb$Instituting clear outcome expectations, granting operational execution autonomy, and holding milestone check-ins.$qb$, $qb$Providing detailed step-by-step formatting rules and reviewing execution work daily to ensure exact compliance.$qb$, $qb$Asking for task volunteers and leaving layout design and execution entirely to their discretion.$qb$, $qb$Creating operational templates personally and delegating only basic routine data entry steps to team members.$qb$, $qb$Handing over tasks completely without scheduling progress reviews to demonstrate trust.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R01-031$qb$, $qb$A5-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You are accountable for a major program but have appointed an experienced program director. Which decision should remain primarily with you?$qb$, $qb$Setting outcome boundaries and risk appetite while giving the director authority over delivery choices within them.$qb$, $qb$Approving every material supplier and staffing decision.$qb$, $qb$Running weekly workstream reviews for direct visibility.$qb$, $qb$Choosing detailed milestone sequence and dependencies.$qb$, $qb$Resolving routine disagreements between workstream leads.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R06-031$qb$, $qb$A5-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$An executive makes a delegated decision that produces a different strategic style from yours but remains within agreed outcomes and risk. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Allow the distinct approach to stand, evaluate it against agreed outcomes, and avoid turning empowerment into replication of your own style.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current model incrementally while retaining its core incentives, assumptions and decision structure.$qb$, $qb$Seek additional stakeholder input but do not establish how contradictory evidence or interests will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a unilateral short-term choice that reduces visible tension but leaves the systemic mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Avoid intervention because changing an established model could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R10-032$qb$, $qb$A5-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A long-standing strategy has become internally inconsistent as external conditions changed, but different businesses still quote parts that favor their agendas. What should leadership do?$qb$, $qb$Rearticulate the strategic choices and boundaries, explicitly retire obsolete elements, align capital and incentives, and remove ambiguity that allows selective interpretation.$qb$, $qb$Address the largest immediate risk and defer less visible enterprise consequences until the next planning cycle.$qb$, $qb$Run a bounded pilot but leave success criteria and the next decision threshold undefined.$qb$, $qb$Ask relevant operating leaders to absorb the issue within existing authority and resources.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that best protects current reported performance and reputation.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R10-031$qb$, $qb$A5-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You delegate a task and the colleague proposes a different method that still meets the requirements. The colleague needs meaningful authority within clear boundaries. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Let them use the method if it stays within the agreed limits and outcome$qb$, $qb$Ask them to explain the method and confirm the expected result$qb$, $qb$Require your method because you delegated the task$qb$, $qb$Take the task back to avoid risk$qb$, $qb$Approve every step before they continue$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R2-031$qb$, $qb$A5-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A delegated task is important, and the colleague has the skill but limited experience with this context. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Delegate the outcome and authority clearly, agree risk-based check-ins, and let them own the work$qb$, $qb$Give them the task with clear success criteria and one or two review points$qb$, $qb$Give detailed instructions for every step$qb$, $qb$Keep the important decisions yourself$qb$, $qb$Do the task yourself because the context is new to them$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R4-031$qb$, $qb$A5-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You delegate a complex task to someone capable, but the work has several stakeholder dependencies. What should you clarify at the start?$qb$, $qb$Expected outcome, decision authority, stakeholder responsibilities, escalation triggers, and meaningful review points$qb$, $qb$Outcome, key stakeholders, and when you want progress updates$qb$, $qb$A detailed step-by-step plan for every interaction$qb$, $qb$That all stakeholder decisions require your approval$qb$, $qb$That they should avoid changing the plan without you$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R4-030$qb$, $qb$A5-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A new teammate completes a task correctly but takes much longer than expected. What is the best coaching response?$qb$, $qb$Ask them to explain their process, then help identify where time can be saved$qb$, $qb$Show them one faster method and let them practice it$qb$, $qb$Tell them they need to work faster next time$qb$, $qb$Take over the task whenever speed matters$qb$, $qb$Compare their speed publicly with an experienced teammate$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R5-030$qb$, $qb$A5-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A junior teammate gives the right answer but relied on a guess. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Ask them to work through the reasoning so they can repeat the judgement reliably$qb$, $qb$Ask what information they would check next time$qb$, $qb$Praise the correct answer and move on$qb$, $qb$Tell them guessing is unacceptable$qb$, $qb$Make future decisions for them$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R6-030$qb$, $qb$A5-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A new teammate solves a problem slowly but correctly. What is the best coaching step?$qb$, $qb$Ask them to explain their method and help them find one safe way to make it faster$qb$, $qb$Review the steps with them and identify one improvement$qb$, $qb$Tell them the expected time and ask them to speed up$qb$, $qb$Show them your method and require them to copy it$qb$, $qb$Take over similar work$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R7-030$qb$, $qb$A5-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A junior teammate completes a task correctly only after asking for help at every step. What is the best coaching response?$qb$, $qb$Review the task with them, identify where confidence is low, and let them handle the next similar task with agreed check-ins$qb$, $qb$Ask them to try the next task first and come to you at defined points$qb$, $qb$Continue answering every question as it comes$qb$, $qb$Give the task to someone more independent$qb$, $qb$Tell them to stop asking questions$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R8-030$qb$, $qb$A5-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A junior teammate asks for the answer before trying a problem themselves. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Ask them to explain what they know and guide them toward the next step$qb$, $qb$Give them a hint and ask them to try first$qb$, $qb$Give the answer so they can learn from it$qb$, $qb$Tell them to solve it alone$qb$, $qb$Take over the problem$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R9-030$qb$, $qb$A5-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A junior colleague fixes an error after you point it out but does not understand why it happened. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Help them identify the cause and the check that would prevent the same error next time$qb$, $qb$Ask them to explain what they will do differently next time$qb$, $qb$Tell them to be more careful$qb$, $qb$Check all their future work yourself$qb$, $qb$Move the task to someone else$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-REF-030$qb$, $qb$A5-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$What coaching strategy is most effective when supporting a junior team member struggling with complex tasks?$qb$, $qb$Remediating technical skill gaps by reviewing work together, modeling shortcuts, and setting review milestones.$qb$, $qb$Providing completed past work samples for the junior staff member to reference during future tasks.$qb$, $qb$Reassigning complex tasks to experienced team members while assigning simpler duties to the junior staff member.$qb$, $qb$Encouraging the junior staff member to work faster during task execution to improve turnaround times.$qb$, $qb$Completing final quality checks personally to ensure client deliverables remain on schedule.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R10-030$qb$, $qb$A5-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A capable employee repeatedly solves problems themselves instead of developing their team. Results remain good, but team dependency is increasing. What coaching response is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Use specific examples to show the dependency risk, explore why they step in, and agree measurable behaviors that shift ownership to the team.$qb$, $qb$Tell them to delegate more and review progress at the next performance cycle.$qb$, $qb$Reduce their workload so they have more time to coach others.$qb$, $qb$Promote a stronger team member who can absorb more responsibility.$qb$, $qb$Accept the style because current results are strong.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R01-030$qb$, $qb$A5-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A respected executive solves difficult problems personally and delivers results, but successors are not developing. How should you coach them?$qb$, $qb$Show how personal problem-solving constrains bench strength, agree outcomes they must enable through others, and review evidence of capability growth over time.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to spend more time mentoring high-potential employees.$qb$, $qb$Reduce their operational workload for development conversations.$qb$, $qb$Nominate their best people for external leadership programs.$qb$, $qb$Leave the approach unchanged because results are strong.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R02-030$qb$, $qb$A5-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A vice president consistently gives solutions whenever team members bring problems, creating speed but little independent judgment. What coaching focus is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Help the leader shift from solving to framing, questioning and setting decision boundaries, then review whether the team increasingly decides well without escalation.$qb$, $qb$Introduce a sensible safeguard but leave the underlying incentives or decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$Run a small corrective action focused mainly on the most visible symptom.$qb$, $qb$Ask individual managers to compensate for the problem through extra effort.$qb$, $qb$Treat the issue as unavoidable and make no material change.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R04-030$qb$, $qb$A5-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A highly capable executive's successors keep leaving because they receive responsibility but little real authority. What should you coach?$qb$, $qb$Help the executive identify where they reclaim decisions, redesign authority boundaries and consequences, and measure whether successors can actually lead outcomes independently.$qb$, $qb$Improve the existing approach incrementally while leaving its main incentives and decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$Ask stakeholders for additional views but do not define how conflicting evidence will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a short-term compromise that reduces visible tension without addressing the underlying mechanism.$qb$, $qb$Continue the status quo because changing it could introduce new uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R05-030$qb$, $qb$A5-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A leader you coach has strong successors on paper, but they are never allowed to represent the function at high-stakes forums. What should you address?$qb$, $qb$Create deliberate exposure with real authority and appropriate support, and examine what risk or identity concern keeps the leader from letting successors operate visibly.$qb$, $qb$For the immediate decision, improve the current approach incrementally while keeping its core incentives, assumptions and decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$At first, seek more stakeholder views but do not establish how contradictory evidence or interests will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$In this case, make a unilateral short-term decision that reduces visible tension but leaves the underlying mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$As an interim response, avoid intervention because changing the current model could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R2-030$qb$, $qb$A5-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A capable junior colleague keeps bringing you decisions they could make themselves. What is the best coaching response?$qb$, $qb$Clarify the decision boundaries, ask for their recommendation, and let them decide within those limits$qb$, $qb$Ask them to bring a recommended option rather than only the problem$qb$, $qb$Continue deciding for them until their confidence improves$qb$, $qb$Tell them to stop asking questions$qb$, $qb$Give them full freedom without clarifying limits$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R8-030$qb$, $qb$A5-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A junior colleague asks you to solve a task they are learning. The goal is to develop independent judgement. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Ask how they would approach it, then guide the gaps in their thinking$qb$, $qb$Give them a useful hint and let them continue$qb$, $qb$Show them the correct solution so they can copy the method$qb$, $qb$Take over the difficult part to save time$qb$, $qb$Tell them to find the answer on their own$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R1-033$qb$, $qb$A5-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A change in your work will delay another team's activity by one day. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Tell the affected team early, explain the impact, and agree the next step$qb$, $qb$Tell them as soon as the new date is fully confirmed$qb$, $qb$Update your manager and let them inform the other team$qb$, $qb$Wait until the other team asks about the delay$qb$, $qb$Say nothing because the delay is only one day$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R3-033$qb$, $qb$A5-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A person affected by your work has not replied to two important questions. What is the best next step?$qb$, $qb$Use another appropriate contact method, explain what is blocked, and agree a response time.$qb$, $qb$Send a clear follow-up showing the impact of the missing answer.$qb$, $qb$Keep sending the same reminder each day.$qb$, $qb$Continue using your own assumptions.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the person contacts you.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R4-033$qb$, $qb$A5-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague in another team will be affected by a change you are making tomorrow. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Tell them before the change, explain the effect, and check what they need$qb$, $qb$Send them a short advance notice about the change$qb$, $qb$Tell your manager and assume the message will reach them$qb$, $qb$Inform them after the change is complete$qb$, $qb$Wait until they notice the effect$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R5-033$qb$, $qb$A5-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You learn that a change may affect a colleague's deadline, but the exact impact is not yet known. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Give an early heads-up, explain what is known, and promise a specific follow-up$qb$, $qb$Tell them there may be an impact and when you expect clarity$qb$, $qb$Wait until the impact is fully confirmed$qb$, $qb$Tell only your manager$qb$, $qb$Say nothing unless the deadline actually moves$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R6-033$qb$, $qb$A5-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague expects a result today, but you discover a dependency may delay it. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Tell them early, explain the dependency, and agree what update or alternative they need$qb$, $qb$Warn them that the result may be late and give the next update time$qb$, $qb$Wait until the delay is certain$qb$, $qb$Tell your manager but not the colleague$qb$, $qb$Say nothing until the original deadline passes$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R7-033$qb$, $qb$A5-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You are changing a process that will make another person's weekly report arrive later. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Discuss the impact before the change and agree how their reporting need will be met$qb$, $qb$Tell them the new timing and ask whether it creates a problem$qb$, $qb$Make the change and send them an update afterward$qb$, $qb$Tell your manager and let them handle the communication$qb$, $qb$Make the change because your process is more efficient$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R8-033$qb$, $qb$A5-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague expects daily updates, but nothing meaningful changes every day. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Agree an update rhythm based on useful changes and decision needs$qb$, $qb$Ask what information they need and how often they use it$qb$, $qb$Keep sending empty daily updates$qb$, $qb$Stop updates until something changes$qb$, $qb$Tell them daily updates are unnecessary$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R9-033$qb$, $qb$A5-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A person asks for more detail in every update, but you are unsure what they actually use. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Ask what decisions they make from the update and tailor the detail to that need$qb$, $qb$Ask which sections they want expanded$qb$, $qb$Add more detail to every update$qb$, $qb$Send the full working file instead$qb$, $qb$Tell them the current update is sufficient$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R10-033$qb$, $qb$A5-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A powerful stakeholder supports your initiative publicly but privately blocks resources because another project benefits their function more. What is the strongest response?$qb$, $qb$Surface the conflicting incentives directly, clarify enterprise consequences, and negotiate an explicit resource decision with the true trade-off visible.$qb$, $qb$Escalate the stakeholder's behavior to their manager with evidence.$qb$, $qb$Reduce your resource request so the stakeholder can support both projects.$qb$, $qb$Continue seeking informal support from other leaders until enough resources appear.$qb$, $qb$Avoid confronting the conflict because the stakeholder's public support is still useful.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R01-033$qb$, $qb$A5-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A regulator wants rapid remediation, customers want uninterrupted service, and investors focus on margin. Your solution cannot maximize all three. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Map obligations, interests and non-negotiables; develop transparent trade-offs; recommend an option; and engage each stakeholder around the decision.$qb$, $qb$Prioritize the regulator because legal authority outweighs other interests.$qb$, $qb$Choose the lowest-cost compliant solution and explain service impact.$qb$, $qb$Ask the board to decide so dissatisfaction sits at the highest level.$qb$, $qb$Communicate different versions of the plan to emphasize what each group values.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R05-033$qb$, $qb$A5-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A large restructuring affects employees, suppliers, local government and customers differently across regions. What is the strongest engagement design?$qb$, $qb$Segment stakeholders by obligations, impact and influence, coordinate core facts and principles, and tailor timing and dialogue without making inconsistent commitments.$qb$, $qb$As an interim response, improve the current approach incrementally while keeping its core incentives, assumptions and decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$For the immediate decision, seek more stakeholder views but do not establish how contradictory evidence or interests will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$At first, make a unilateral short-term decision that reduces visible tension but leaves the underlying mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$In this case, avoid intervention because changing the current model could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R2-033$qb$, $qb$A5-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A stakeholder asks for a weekly report that takes significant effort, but you suspect they use only one part of it. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Understand the decision need, agree the minimum useful information and cadence, and redesign the report accordingly$qb$, $qb$Ask which sections they actually use and simplify the report$qb$, $qb$Continue the full report because the stakeholder requested it$qb$, $qb$Stop the report until they explain why they need it$qb$, $qb$Send raw data instead of the report$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R4-033$qb$, $qb$A5-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A stakeholder keeps requesting additional detail after work has started, creating repeated rework. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Understand the decision need, agree what detail is essential, and create a clearer requirement and change process for future requests$qb$, $qb$Discuss the repeated additions and agree what should be fixed before work begins$qb$, $qb$Continue adding detail to maintain the relationship$qb$, $qb$Refuse all additional detail after work starts$qb$, $qb$Escalate the stakeholder to management$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R1-032$qb$, $qb$A5-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your team has two useful tasks, but only one clearly supports this month's main goal. Which should take priority?$qb$, $qb$The task that contributes more directly to the main goal$qb$, $qb$The task with the clearer measurable output$qb$, $qb$The task that can be completed more quickly$qb$, $qb$The task requested most recently$qb$, $qb$The task the team enjoys more$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R3-032$qb$, $qb$A5-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A popular weekly activity takes time away from a newly agreed team priority. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Compare its contribution with the new priority and reduce, defer, or stop it if it no longer earns the time.$qb$, $qb$Ask whether the activity can be shortened so more time goes to the priority.$qb$, $qb$Keep both activities and ask the team to work faster.$qb$, $qb$Protect the popular activity because people enjoy it.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the new priority until the next planning cycle.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R4-032$qb$, $qb$A5-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your team is busy with several useful activities, but one has little connection to the current goal. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Reduce or stop the low-value activity and redirect effort to the goal$qb$, $qb$Review whether the activity still has a useful purpose$qb$, $qb$Keep it because the team has already invested time$qb$, $qb$Continue until the next planning cycle$qb$, $qb$Add more people so all activities can continue$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R6-032$qb$, $qb$A5-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A new request is useful but would delay work tied directly to the team's main goal. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Protect the goal-critical work and negotiate timing for the new request$qb$, $qb$Explain the conflict and offer a later delivery for the new request$qb$, $qb$Accept both and ask the team to work faster$qb$, $qb$Do the new request first because someone asked for it$qb$, $qb$Drop the goal-critical work without discussion$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R7-032$qb$, $qb$A5-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your team has capacity for only one of two improvements. One directly supports the current goal; the other is easier to deliver. Which should you choose?$qb$, $qb$The improvement that contributes more to the current goal$qb$, $qb$The goal-linked improvement after confirming its expected impact$qb$, $qb$The easier improvement because it can show a quick win$qb$, $qb$Split effort between both$qb$, $qb$Choose the one most people prefer$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R8-032$qb$, $qb$A5-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your team receives a request that is useful but does not support the current quarter's main outcome. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Assess its value against committed priorities and negotiate timing if it would distract from the main outcome$qb$, $qb$Explain current priorities and offer a later time$qb$, $qb$Accept it because it is useful$qb$, $qb$Add it to the team's workload without changing anything$qb$, $qb$Drop a priority task without discussion$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R9-032$qb$, $qb$A5-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A proposed task would improve a minor internal process but delay an important customer commitment. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Protect the customer commitment and schedule the internal improvement when capacity allows$qb$, $qb$Explain the trade-off and defer the internal task$qb$, $qb$Try to do both at the same time$qb$, $qb$Do the internal improvement because it is quick$qb$, $qb$Delay the customer work without discussion$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-REF-032$qb$, $qb$A5-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$How does an operations lead align daily team routines with new corporate strategic priorities?$qb$, $qb$Translating strategic goals into practical workflow adjustments and setting measurable operational targets.$qb$, $qb$Reminding team members during group meetings to keep strategic priorities in mind during task execution.$qb$, $qb$Awaiting specific step-by-step operational instructions from executive management before altering team workflows.$qb$, $qb$Sharing corporate strategy announcements in team communication channels to promote general awareness.$qb$, $qb$Focusing team effort exclusively on core local targets, assuming strategy is managed by executive leads.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R10-032$qb$, $qb$A5-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A division has five strategic priorities, but investment and leadership attention are spread almost evenly across twelve initiatives. What is the best corrective action?$qb$, $qb$Translate the priorities into explicit resource choices, stop or defer misaligned work, and make the consequences visible to sponsors.$qb$, $qb$Remind leaders that the five priorities should receive more attention.$qb$, $qb$Rank all twelve initiatives and continue them in priority order.$qb$, $qb$Ask each function to identify which initiatives it considers strategic.$qb$, $qb$Increase the strategic-priority list so more active initiatives are formally included.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R01-032$qb$, $qb$A5-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A profitable legacy business funds current earnings while a new model is strategically necessary but initially dilutive. What is the strongest alignment approach?$qb$, $qb$Define the transition thesis, specify how the legacy business funds and enables the future model, and use distinct measures and milestones for both horizons.$qb$, $qb$Set one common growth target so both are judged consistently.$qb$, $qb$Prioritize the new model immediately because strategy outweighs earnings.$qb$, $qb$Protect the legacy business until the new model proves profitability.$qb$, $qb$Let each business pursue its own objectives and reconcile at year-end.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R03-032$qb$, $qb$A5-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A move upmarket grows revenue but increasingly depends on discounts that weaken the premium position the board approved. What should leadership do?$qb$, $qb$Reconcile growth with strategic position by examining customer economics, discount drivers and resource choices, then reset boundaries and measures.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current approach incrementally while keeping its core design and incentives intact.$qb$, $qb$Ask the main stakeholders for more input but do not establish how conflicting evidence will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a unilateral short-term decision primarily to restore speed.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the status quo because changing it could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R05-032$qb$, $qb$A5-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A strategy depends on serving fewer, higher-value customers, but sales incentives still reward account volume. What should leadership do?$qb$, $qb$Align incentives, capacity and customer-selection rules with the strategic choice so teams are not rewarded for behavior that undermines the intended model.$qb$, $qb$In this case, apply the closest existing precedent with an added safeguard, even though the precedent does not fully address the situation.$qb$, $qb$As an interim response, escalate for a higher-level ruling before fully working through the competing assumptions and consequences.$qb$, $qb$For the immediate decision, choose the quickest workable compromise to restore momentum without addressing the underlying driver.$qb$, $qb$At first, protect the current target or position even if the cost is transferred elsewhere.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R2-032$qb$, $qb$A5-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your team has several good improvement ideas but limited capacity. How should you choose?$qb$, $qb$Prioritize ideas by contribution to the agreed goals, expected value, effort, and risk$qb$, $qb$Rank ideas by expected impact on the main goals$qb$, $qb$Choose the easiest ideas to create quick wins$qb$, $qb$Choose the ideas proposed by senior stakeholders$qb$, $qb$Try to start all ideas so none are rejected$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R4-032$qb$, $qb$A5-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A senior stakeholder requests useful work that would materially delay an agreed strategic priority. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Explain the trade-off against the agreed objective, assess relative value, and negotiate timing or scope rather than simply accepting both$qb$, $qb$Show the impact on the current priority and propose a later delivery$qb$, $qb$Accept the senior request and move the existing priority$qb$, $qb$Try to complete both without changing expectations$qb$, $qb$Reject the senior request immediately$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R1-046$qb$, $qb$A5-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A task is stuck because two teams each think the other team owns the next step. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Bring both sides together, clarify the handoff, and agree one owner$qb$, $qb$Contact the other team and ask who can take the next step$qb$, $qb$Escalate the delay to both managers immediately$qb$, $qb$Complete the other team's step yourself without agreement$qb$, $qb$Wait until one team accepts responsibility$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-REF-046$qb$, $qb$A5-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$What is the most effective strategy to eliminate recurring workflow delays between interfacing departments?$qb$, $qb$Co-designing shared workflow processes and binding service-level agreements (SLAs) collaboratively with partner leads.$qb$, $qb$Instructing internal team members to submit task requests earlier to accommodate partner department delays.$qb$, $qb$Escalating recurring inter-departmental delays to senior executives to enforce compliance with schedules.$qb$, $qb$Sending regular reminder notifications to partner department approvers whenever task deadlines approach.$qb$, $qb$Bypassing standard cross-departmental channels by requesting emergency execution overrides from supervisors.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R10-046$qb$, $qb$A5-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Another team needs information your team already has, but nobody owns the handoff. Both teams meet local measures while the shared result suffers. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Agree who will provide the information now and define the handoff for future cases$qb$, $qb$Send the information and tell both teams that ownership needs clarification$qb$, $qb$Ask the other team to submit a formal request$qb$, $qb$Wait for a manager to assign an owner$qb$, $qb$Keep the information within your team because no handoff is defined$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R2-046$qb$, $qb$A5-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Two teams meet their own targets, but their handoff creates repeated delays for customers. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Map the shared flow, agree a common outcome and handoff measures, and redesign ownership across the boundary$qb$, $qb$Bring both teams together to identify where the handoff creates delay$qb$, $qb$Ask each team to improve its own target performance further$qb$, $qb$Escalate the slower team to management$qb$, $qb$Leave the handoff unchanged because both teams meet their targets$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R3-046$qb$, $qb$A5-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your team changes a file format and the receiving team can no longer use it easily. Both teams currently meet their own measures even though the shared outcome is suffering. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Work with the receiving team to agree a format that meets both sides' needs$qb$, $qb$Ask what part of the new format causes difficulty and adjust it$qb$, $qb$Send instructions showing how to convert the file$qb$, $qb$Tell them the new format is now standard$qb$, $qb$Expect them to adapt on their own$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R6-046$qb$, $qb$A5-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your team and another team both collect the same customer data separately. Both teams meet their own measures while the shared customer result is poor. What is the best improvement?$qb$, $qb$Agree a shared source, ownership, and update process so the data is collected once$qb$, $qb$Compare the two collections and decide which can become the main source$qb$, $qb$Ask the other team to stop collecting the data$qb$, $qb$Keep both collections and reconcile differences$qb$, $qb$Ignore the duplication because each team has its own process$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R3-045$qb$, $qb$A5-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A new teammate raises a risk in a planning meeting that others have not mentioned. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Thank them for raising it, examine the evidence, and include the risk in the plan if it is credible.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to explain the risk and what could happen.$qb$, $qb$Tell them the plan has already been reviewed by experienced people.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to discuss the concern privately after the meeting.$qb$, $qb$Dismiss the concern because nobody else raised it.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R10-045$qb$, $qb$A5-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A teammate points out a problem in your plan during a group discussion. The concern challenges a plan supported by senior people. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Thank them, explore the concern, and change the plan if the evidence supports it$qb$, $qb$Ask them to explain the concern and consider it$qb$, $qb$Defend the plan so the group does not lose confidence$qb$, $qb$Tell them to raise concerns privately next time$qb$, $qb$Dismiss the concern because the plan is already approved$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-031$qb$, $qb$A5-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are leading a sub-project and need to delegate the creation of a weekly tracking dashboard to a reliable team member. How do you structure delegation?$qb$, $qb$Define expected dashboard outcomes, agree on tracking milestones, provide necessary access, and grant autonomy while maintaining scheduled check-ins.$qb$, $qb$Assign the task with detailed step-by-step formatting instructions and review their work daily to ensure exact compliance.$qb$, $qb$Ask for a volunteer to take on dashboard creation and leave the layout design entirely to their discretion.$qb$, $qb$Create the dashboard template yourself and delegate only the weekly data entry steps to the team member.$qb$, $qb$Delegate the task fully without scheduling check-ins to demonstrate complete trust in their capabilities.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-077$qb$, $qb$A5-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You delegate a recurring dashboard to a capable colleague. They ask you to approve every small design choice even though the output requirements are clear. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Reconfirm the outcome and decision boundaries, ask them to make routine choices independently, and agree only a few meaningful review points.$qb$, $qb$Continue answering questions so the first few dashboards meet your preferences.$qb$, $qb$Give them a completed template and require them to follow it exactly.$qb$, $qb$Take back the dashboard design and delegate only data entry.$qb$, $qb$Tell them not to contact you again until the dashboard is complete.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1133$qb$, $qb$A5-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague owns a delegated internal survey and wants to change the reminder schedule because response patterns show the current reminders are poorly timed. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm any fixed communication constraints, review their evidence, and let them adjust and evaluate the reminder schedule within their delegated ownership.$qb$, $qb$Choose the new reminder times yourself.$qb$, $qb$Keep the original schedule because it was approved at project start.$qb$, $qb$Require manager approval for every reminder.$qb$, $qb$Take the survey communications back from them.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-123$qb$, $qb$A5-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You give a colleague ownership of a supplier review. They bring you three reasonable options and ask you to choose, although the decision is within the agreed scope. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Ask for their recommendation and reasoning, confirm the decision boundaries, and let them make the choice if it remains within those boundaries.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option you prefer so the review can proceed quickly.$qb$, $qb$Tell them to decide but do not discuss their reasoning.$qb$, $qb$Take back the supplier review because they are not ready.$qb$, $qb$Ask a more experienced colleague to choose.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1271$qb$, $qb$A5-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague owns a delegated customer survey and wants to remove one question that almost nobody answers and that has never been used in decisions. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the question has no regulatory or stakeholder requirement, review the evidence, and let them remove it while monitoring whether any useful information is lost.$qb$, $qb$Remove the question yourself.$qb$, $qb$Keep it because the survey was originally approved with it.$qb$, $qb$Ask every stakeholder to approve the deletion.$qb$, $qb$Take survey design back under your control.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1501$qb$, $qb$A5-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague owns a delegated monthly meeting and wants to stop inviting a participant who has no decisions or actions in the last six meetings. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm there is no governance reason for attendance, then let the owner adjust the invite list and monitor whether the participant still receives any information they need.$qb$, $qb$Remove the participant yourself.$qb$, $qb$Keep everyone invited because broad visibility is useful.$qb$, $qb$Ask all attendees to vote.$qb$, $qb$Take meeting ownership back.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1547$qb$, $qb$A5-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague owns a delegated status report and wants to remove a section that has been blank for six months because the underlying project phase ended. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm no stakeholder or governance need remains, then let them remove the obsolete section and update the report structure.$qb$, $qb$Remove it yourself.$qb$, $qb$Keep it because the report template was approved.$qb$, $qb$Ask every recipient for formal permission.$qb$, $qb$Take report ownership back.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1593$qb$, $qb$A5-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague owns a delegated monthly dashboard and wants to replace a chart that users consistently misread with a clearer table. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the same decision information is preserved, review the user evidence, and let them test the clearer format within their ownership.$qb$, $qb$Choose the replacement format yourself.$qb$, $qb$Keep the chart because dashboards should be visual.$qb$, $qb$Ask every user to approve the change.$qb$, $qb$Take dashboard design back.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-167$qb$, $qb$A5-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You delegate an important analysis to a capable colleague. They propose a method different from yours, but it appears technically sound. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Judge their method against the required outcome, risks, and constraints; if it meets them, let them proceed and review the result at agreed checkpoints.$qb$, $qb$Let them use their method but ask them to include key parts of your usual approach as well.$qb$, $qb$Allow the method only if they send you frequent updates so you can intervene quickly.$qb$, $qb$Require your method because consistency is important when you remain accountable for the outcome.$qb$, $qb$Take the analysis back because different methods create unnecessary quality risk.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-213$qb$, $qb$A5-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You delegate a recurring responsibility to a colleague, but other team members continue bringing all related decisions to you. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Redirect routine decisions to the colleague consistently, clarify their authority to the team, and remain available only for agreed exceptions.$qb$, $qb$Tell team members privately that the colleague now handles the responsibility.$qb$, $qb$Let the colleague do the work but continue making decisions when people come to you.$qb$, $qb$Ask the colleague to copy you on every decision so the team remains confident.$qb$, $qb$Take the responsibility back because the team is clearly more comfortable with you.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2375$qb$, $qb$A5-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A delegated workstream owner wants to swap two milestones to reduce rework while preserving the final deadline and dependencies. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Ask them to show the dependency impact and, if constraints remain protected, let them make and communicate the sequencing change.$qb$, $qb$Implement a practical interim adjustment to address immediate operational needs while monitoring results.$qb$, $qb$Maintain existing procedures temporarily until formal company-wide guidance is published.$qb$, $qb$Escalate the issue to departmental leadership without first gathering supporting data or options.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the operational discrepancy and proceed with business as usual.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2559$qb$, $qb$A5-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A delegated owner wants to shorten a weekly review by moving routine metrics to a dashboard and keeping live time for exceptions. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm decisions, dependencies and governance remain covered, then let the owner pilot the shorter exception-focused review.$qb$, $qb$Provide detailed step-by-step instructions and review every intermediate draft closely.$qb$, $qb$Delegate the entire task without setting clear milestones, check-ins, or success criteria.$qb$, $qb$Take back the task and complete it yourself to prevent delivery delays.$qb$, $qb$Reassign the task to another team member without explaining the rationale.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2827$qb$, $qb$A5-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague owns a delegated report and wants to change its internal working template while keeping all required outputs unchanged. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the required outputs and controls are preserved, then let them choose and test the working template as part of their ownership.$qb$, $qb$Approve every template field yourself.$qb$, $qb$Keep the old template for consistency.$qb$, $qb$Ask all stakeholders to approve the internal template.$qb$, $qb$Take report design back under your control.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-305$qb$, $qb$A5-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague you delegated to makes a reasonable decision you would not have made, and the result is acceptable. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Support the decision, discuss the reasoning and outcome for learning, and avoid replacing their judgment merely because your preferred approach was different.$qb$, $qb$Tell them the result is acceptable but explain how you would have decided differently.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to use your approach next time so decisions remain consistent.$qb$, $qb$Reduce their decision authority until their judgment matches yours more closely.$qb$, $qb$Take over similar decisions because you remain accountable for the work.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-351$qb$, $qb$A5-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A delegated task is progressing well, but the colleague asks you to attend every stakeholder meeting 'just in case.' What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Clarify which situations genuinely require your involvement, let them lead routine stakeholder meetings, and remain available for agreed escalation points.$qb$, $qb$Attend but let the colleague do most of the talking.$qb$, $qb$Join only the first half of each meeting.$qb$, $qb$Tell them they must handle all meetings alone from now on.$qb$, $qb$Take stakeholder communication back because they are not yet confident.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-397$qb$, $qb$A5-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague successfully completes a delegated task but asks you to present the result to senior stakeholders because you are more experienced. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Help them prepare, let them present the work they own, and attend only as support for questions or escalation that genuinely require your role.$qb$, $qb$Present jointly and handle the most important sections yourself.$qb$, $qb$Present it for them this time and promise they can present the next one.$qb$, $qb$Ask another experienced colleague to present so the result receives proper credibility.$qb$, $qb$Take back stakeholder communication because senior presentations carry too much risk.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-443$qb$, $qb$A5-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague handling delegated work encounters a problem outside the boundaries you originally discussed. They ask what to do. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Review the new risk together, clarify whether it changes their decision authority, agree the expanded or revised boundary, and let them continue ownership where appropriate.$qb$, $qb$Tell them exactly what to do for this problem and keep the original boundaries unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Take the whole task back because the situation is now different.$qb$, $qb$Tell them to use their judgment even though the new problem was outside the agreed boundary.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to wait until you can personally handle the issue.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-489$qb$, $qb$A5-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You delegate a task and the colleague completes it early, then asks whether they should make a small related improvement that is within the agreed scope. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Ask for their reasoning and expected benefit, then let them decide within the agreed boundary if the improvement does not create new material risk.$qb$, $qb$Approve the improvement yourself because any scope change should come back to you.$qb$, $qb$Tell them to stop because the original task is already complete.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to wait until you have time to review the improvement in detail.$qb$, $qb$Take back the work so you can decide what additional improvements are worthwhile.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-535$qb$, $qb$A5-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague you delegated to proposes spending a small amount within the budget to solve a problem. You had not discussed spending authority explicitly. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Clarify the spending boundary and relevant policy, review their reasoning, and if appropriate give them explicit authority for this and similar low-risk decisions going forward.$qb$, $qb$Approve this purchase yourself and leave future spending decisions unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Tell them delegated work does not include any spending decisions.$qb$, $qb$Let them spend it because the amount is small.$qb$, $qb$Take back budget-related parts of the task permanently.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-673$qb$, $qb$A5-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague who owns a delegated workstream wants to invite a specialist to one workshop using resources already approved for the work. They ask you to make the invitation because you are more senior. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm that using the specialist fits the workstream's scope and approved resources, then ask the colleague to make and manage the invitation as part of their ownership.$qb$, $qb$Make the introduction yourself, then let the colleague manage the specialist afterward.$qb$, $qb$Approve the specialist but ask to be copied on all communication.$qb$, $qb$Tell the colleague to continue without the specialist because external coordination should remain with you.$qb$, $qb$Take over the workshop planning because involving a specialist increases complexity.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-765$qb$, $qb$A5-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague owns a delegated task and wants to change the order in which two internal reviewers are consulted. The final controls and deadline remain unchanged. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Check that no dependency or policy requires the original order, then let the colleague choose the sequence and own the coordination.$qb$, $qb$Approve the change but ask them to confirm with you before each review.$qb$, $qb$Keep the original order because it was in the plan.$qb$, $qb$Ask both reviewers which order they prefer and decide yourself.$qb$, $qb$Take back review coordination because changes can create risk.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-949$qb$, $qb$A5-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague responsible for a delegated training session wants to select refreshments from an approved supplier within the agreed budget. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Ask for their reasoning and let them resolve it within the existing boundary, escalating only if new risk or authority limits arise.$qb$, $qb$Settle it yourself.$qb$, $qb$Require approval for every credit.$qb$, $qb$Tell them never to offer credits.$qb$, $qb$Take the vendor relationship back.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-030$qb$, $qb$A5-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A junior staff member assigned to your project team struggles with formatting complex client reports, taking twice as long as expected. How do you coach them?$qb$, $qb$Review recent work together, walk through practical formatting shortcuts, co-create a clear checklist, and schedule a follow-up review.$qb$, $qb$Provide them with completed past report templates to reference whenever they encounter formatting difficulties.$qb$, $qb$Reassign report formatting tasks to a more experienced team member while giving the junior staff member simpler assignments.$qb$, $qb$Encourage them to work faster during client report preparation to improve turnaround times.$qb$, $qb$Take over the final formatting pass yourself to ensure client deliverables remain on schedule.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-076$qb$, $qb$A5-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A capable junior employee keeps making avoidable mistakes when work becomes busy, although their normal work is strong. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Review the busy-period examples with them, identify the failure pattern, coach a practical checking method, and set a short follow-up to see whether the habit improves.$qb$, $qb$Tell them to slow down whenever workload increases.$qb$, $qb$Check their work yourself during busy periods.$qb$, $qb$Reduce their workload permanently.$qb$, $qb$Give them a written warning so they take accuracy more seriously.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1132$qb$, $qb$A5-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team member often asks you to confirm their analysis even when the evidence is clear. Their conclusions are usually correct. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Review what makes them seek confirmation, define the evidence threshold for independent conclusions, and gradually stop rechecking routine cases while remaining available for genuine uncertainty.$qb$, $qb$Continue checking because it ensures quality.$qb$, $qb$Tell them never to ask you again.$qb$, $qb$Require them to ask another colleague instead.$qb$, $qb$Reduce the complexity of their work.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1178$qb$, $qb$A5-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team member produces a weak first draft and asks you to rewrite it because you can do it faster. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Identify the main gaps, coach them on a clear revision approach, and have them improve the draft themselves while setting a review point appropriate to the deadline.$qb$, $qb$Rewrite it because speed matters.$qb$, $qb$Return it with a general instruction to improve quality.$qb$, $qb$Give the task to a stronger teammate.$qb$, $qb$Approve the weak draft so they learn from stakeholder feedback.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-122$qb$, $qb$A5-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team member's work is technically good, but they often solve problems themselves instead of helping less-experienced colleagues learn. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Recognize their expertise, explain the broader team need, coach them on when to guide rather than take over, and set an expectation for knowledge transfer.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to run one training session.$qb$, $qb$Tell junior colleagues to ask someone else for help.$qb$, $qb$Reduce the expert's own work so they can teach full-time.$qb$, $qb$Accept the behavior because fast problem resolution is valuable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1316$qb$, $qb$A5-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team member asks you to solve a customer issue they are authorized to handle because the customer sounds unhappy. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Help them assess the customer's concern and risk, reinforce their authority, and have them handle the case while remaining available if it crosses an escalation threshold.$qb$, $qb$Take over because unhappy customers are sensitive.$qb$, $qb$Tell them to handle it alone without discussion.$qb$, $qb$Ask another experienced employee to take it.$qb$, $qb$Escalate every unhappy customer to a manager.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1454$qb$, $qb$A5-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A capable team member asks you to make a routine decision for them because a senior stakeholder is copied on the email. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Review the routine decision and authority with them, coach how to communicate it confidently, and have them make the decision despite the senior stakeholder being copied.$qb$, $qb$Take the decision because a senior stakeholder is watching.$qb$, $qb$Ask the senior stakeholder to decide.$qb$, $qb$Tell the employee to decide without any discussion.$qb$, $qb$Remove their authority for sensitive emails.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1500$qb$, $qb$A5-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team member asks you to choose between two routine suppliers even though both are within their delegated authority and they have the comparison data. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Ask them to state the relevant criteria and recommendation, coach any gap in the comparison, and let them make the choice within their authority.$qb$, $qb$Choose the supplier you prefer.$qb$, $qb$Tell them to pick the cheaper supplier.$qb$, $qb$Ask procurement to decide.$qb$, $qb$Take supplier choice out of their delegated role.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1592$qb$, $qb$A5-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team member asks you to decide whether to grant a routine extension that is clearly within their delegated policy limit. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Ask them to apply the relevant criteria and state their decision, coach any gap, and let them act within their authority.$qb$, $qb$Decide for them because extensions affect customers.$qb$, $qb$Tell them to approve every request within the limit.$qb$, $qb$Ask a senior colleague to decide.$qb$, $qb$Remove extension authority from them.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-166$qb$, $qb$A5-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A capable employee completes work correctly but asks you to approve every step before moving forward. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Clarify which decisions they can make independently, ask them to bring a recommended next step rather than a request for permission, and reduce checks as their judgment proves reliable.$qb$, $qb$Reduce the number of checkpoints and reassure them that you trust their ability.$qb$, $qb$Tell them they no longer need approval on routine work and should make their own decisions.$qb$, $qb$Continue checking every step because the arrangement is producing accurate work.$qb$, $qb$Take the task back until they become more confident in another area.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-212$qb$, $qb$A5-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A normally strong team member has missed two deadlines and says they are 'fine' when you ask generally how things are going. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Meet privately, describe the two missed deadlines specifically, ask open questions about what has changed, listen, then agree expectations, support, and a review point.$qb$, $qb$Ask again whether everything is fine and remind them that deadlines are important.$qb$, $qb$Reduce their workload immediately because two misses suggest overload.$qb$, $qb$Give a formal warning so the decline does not continue.$qb$, $qb$Move important work to other team members until their performance improves.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2826$qb$, $qb$A5-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team member makes technically sound decisions but spends too long seeking reassurance from several colleagues first. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Review a recent decision, clarify what evidence was already sufficient, coach a stopping rule for consultation, and let them decide once the threshold is met.$qb$, $qb$Tell colleagues to stop answering their questions.$qb$, $qb$Set a strict time limit for every decision.$qb$, $qb$Make the decisions yourself to save time.$qb$, $qb$Accept the delay because consultation improves quality.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-304$qb$, $qb$A5-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team member asks you to review a routine piece of work that they have completed correctly several times before. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Ask what specifically they are uncertain about, reinforce the quality criteria, and encourage them to complete the final check and submit independently if no new risk is present.$qb$, $qb$Review it quickly this time and remind them that future routine work does not need review.$qb$, $qb$Tell them you trust them and refuse to look at the work.$qb$, $qb$Continue reviewing because it only takes a few minutes and prevents errors.$qb$, $qb$Ask another senior colleague to review it instead.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-350$qb$, $qb$A5-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team member completes a task correctly but cannot explain why they chose their approach. You want them to handle more complex work soon. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Ask them to walk through their reasoning, explore alternatives and trade-offs together, and use a similar next task to practice making the decision logic explicit.$qb$, $qb$Tell them the correct reasoning behind the approach they happened to choose.$qb$, $qb$Give them more complex work and see whether their judgment develops through experience.$qb$, $qb$Keep them on routine tasks until they can explain their choices without help.$qb$, $qb$Focus only on whether their output remains correct.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-396$qb$, $qb$A5-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team member asks for more responsibility but recently needed significant help on a similar task. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Discuss what they learned from the earlier task, give them a meaningful next responsibility with clear boundaries and check-ins, and increase autonomy as their judgment proves reliable.$qb$, $qb$Give them the full responsibility because motivation is important for development.$qb$, $qb$Keep them on current work until they can complete similar tasks without any help.$qb$, $qb$Give them only administrative parts of the larger task.$qb$, $qb$Tell them they are not ready and revisit the request at the next annual review.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-442$qb$, $qb$A5-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team member completes work accurately but takes much longer than peers because they repeatedly recheck low-risk details. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Review where their checking time goes, clarify which controls are essential, coach a risk-based checking approach, and compare quality and speed on the next tasks.$qb$, $qb$Tell them they need to work faster while maintaining the same quality.$qb$, $qb$Remove some checks from their checklist without discussing why.$qb$, $qb$Give them fewer tasks so they have enough time to check everything.$qb$, $qb$Accept the slower pace because accuracy is more important than efficiency.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-580$qb$, $qb$A5-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team member completes assigned work well but rarely identifies risks until you point them out. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Use a recent task to ask what could go wrong, coach a simple risk-identification method, and require them to bring likely risks and mitigations with the next plan.$qb$, $qb$Give them a standard list of risks to check on every task.$qb$, $qb$Continue identifying risks yourself because their delivery is otherwise strong.$qb$, $qb$Tell them they need to become more proactive about risk.$qb$, $qb$Assign risk identification to a more experienced teammate.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-672$qb$, $qb$A5-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team member produces a weak recommendation but has gathered good evidence. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Ask them to connect the evidence to the decision, challenge assumptions and alternatives together, and have them revise the recommendation rather than giving them your answer.$qb$, $qb$Tell them the recommendation you would make and explain why.$qb$, $qb$Approve the evidence section and rewrite the recommendation yourself.$qb$, $qb$Tell them to collect more evidence even though the current evidence is adequate.$qb$, $qb$Move recommendation work to a more experienced colleague.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-718$qb$, $qb$A5-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team member makes good decisions but struggles to explain them when challenged by stakeholders. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Practice a recent decision with them, coach a simple evidence-and-trade-off explanation, and let them handle the next stakeholder challenge with you available for support.$qb$, $qb$Attend all stakeholder discussions and explain their decisions for them.$qb$, $qb$Tell them to prepare more detailed notes before every meeting.$qb$, $qb$Move stakeholder communication to someone more confident.$qb$, $qb$Tell them their decisions are good, so they should simply be more confident.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-764$qb$, $qb$A5-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team member brings you a problem and proposes a solution that would work but is more complex than necessary. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Ask them to explain the objective and constraints, explore a simpler alternative together, and let them refine the solution so they learn to match complexity to the problem.$qb$, $qb$Tell them the simpler solution and ask them to use it.$qb$, $qb$Approve their solution because it will work.$qb$, $qb$Reject it and ask them to come back with something simpler.$qb$, $qb$Take over the problem because simplifying solutions requires experience.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-856$qb$, $qb$A5-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team member keeps asking you to review their emails before they send them, although recent emails have been good. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Review what they are still uncertain about, agree clear quality criteria, and reduce pre-review so they send routine messages independently while escalating genuinely sensitive cases.$qb$, $qb$Keep reviewing because it only takes a few minutes.$qb$, $qb$Stop reviewing immediately and tell them to trust themselves.$qb$, $qb$Give them standard email templates for every situation.$qb$, $qb$Ask another teammate to review instead.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-033$qb$, $qb$A5-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A internal department head demands custom weekly reports that require significant manual work from your team. How do you manage this stakeholder request?$qb$, $qb$Schedule an alignment conversation to understand their underlying decision-making needs, propose a streamlined standard metric view, and agree on frequency.$qb$, $qb$Fulfill the custom weekly report request as asked to build strong inter-departmental relationships.$qb$, $qb$Politely decline the request, citing limited team capacity and standard reporting policies.$qb$, $qb$Escalate the request to your supervisor to negotiate reporting boundaries with the department head.$qb$, $qb$Provide the custom report bi-weekly instead of weekly without discussing schedule adjustments.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1181$qb$, $qb$A5-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A stakeholder asks for a weekly call because they find the project dashboard difficult to interpret. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Understand which information is unclear, improve or explain the dashboard where practical, and agree a call cadence focused on decisions rather than permanently duplicating status reporting.$qb$, $qb$Schedule the weekly call indefinitely.$qb$, $qb$Tell them to learn the dashboard.$qb$, $qb$Send them a separate manual report every week.$qb$, $qb$Ask another stakeholder to explain the dashboard to them.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-125$qb$, $qb$A5-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A stakeholder regularly sends urgent requests through informal messages, bypassing the agreed prioritization process. Some requests are genuinely important. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Discuss the pattern, understand why they bypass the process, agree a fast path for genuine urgency, and make trade-offs visible so normal priorities remain controlled.$qb$, $qb$Tell them to use the official process every time.$qb$, $qb$Continue accepting the informal requests because the stakeholder is important.$qb$, $qb$Ignore informal messages until they use the official channel.$qb$, $qb$Escalate the stakeholder to your manager.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1319$qb$, $qb$A5-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A stakeholder says they want to review every customer communication because one previous message contained an error. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Identify what control failed, strengthen review for material or higher-risk communications, and agree visibility that restores confidence without making the stakeholder a bottleneck for routine messages.$qb$, $qb$Send every message to them for approval.$qb$, $qb$Refuse because communication review is not their role.$qb$, $qb$Copy them after every message is sent.$qb$, $qb$Ask the sponsor to tell them to trust the team.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1457$qb$, $qb$A5-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A stakeholder asks for daily detailed updates because one major dependency was previously communicated too late. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Identify which dependency changes actually need the stakeholder's attention and create timely exception updates instead of a daily detailed report.$qb$, $qb$Send a full detailed report every day.$qb$, $qb$Refuse any extra reporting.$qb$, $qb$Send the project tracker daily without explanation.$qb$, $qb$Ask the sponsor to manage the stakeholder.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1595$qb$, $qb$A5-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A stakeholder asks for a weekly meeting after missing one important decision that was recorded in the project log but not highlighted to them. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Identify which decisions require active notification, improve targeted alerts or checkpoints, and add meetings only where live discussion is genuinely needed.$qb$, $qb$Schedule the weekly meeting permanently.$qb$, $qb$Tell them to read the project log more carefully.$qb$, $qb$Send them every project-log update.$qb$, $qb$Ask the sponsor to decide whether they need meetings.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-169$qb$, $qb$A5-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Two stakeholders want incompatible outcomes from the same project, and neither realizes that satisfying one request makes the other harder to achieve. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Surface the conflict early, clarify the priorities and decision rights, explore trade-offs with both stakeholders, and document the agreed direction.$qb$, $qb$Meet each stakeholder separately and try to find a compromise that keeps both reasonably satisfied.$qb$, $qb$Follow the request from the more senior stakeholder because their priority will ultimately prevail.$qb$, $qb$Ask the project team to choose the option that is easiest to deliver.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the conflict creates an actual delivery problem so there is a concrete issue to escalate.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-261$qb$, $qb$A5-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A stakeholder who was not involved early in a project now objects to a design choice that is expensive to change. Their concern is legitimate. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Understand the concern and impact, show the current cost and constraints, explore lower-cost ways to address the underlying need, and agree the trade-off with the relevant decision owners.$qb$, $qb$Explain that the design was already approved and offer to consider the concern in a future phase.$qb$, $qb$Make the requested change because the stakeholder should have been consulted earlier.$qb$, $qb$Ask the project sponsor to decide whether the late stakeholder has enough authority to force the change.$qb$, $qb$Continue with the design because changing it now would be inefficient.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-353$qb$, $qb$A5-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A stakeholder agrees to a compromise in a meeting but later tells their team to continue with the original request. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Speak with the stakeholder promptly, refer to the agreed compromise, understand what changed, and reconfirm or revise the decision before teams continue in different directions.$qb$, $qb$Send the meeting minutes to their team and tell them to follow the documented decision.$qb$, $qb$Escalate the contradiction to the project sponsor.$qb$, $qb$Let both teams continue temporarily until the stakeholder clarifies their position.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the later instruction because the meeting agreement is formally recorded.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-399$qb$, $qb$A5-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A stakeholder requests weekly detailed updates, but the team says producing them takes time away from delivery. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Understand what decisions or reassurance the stakeholder needs, agree a lighter update containing those signals, and set a cadence that provides visibility without unnecessary reporting effort.$qb$, $qb$Keep the weekly report because stakeholder confidence is important.$qb$, $qb$Send the stakeholder the team's internal dashboard instead of preparing a report.$qb$, $qb$Reduce updates to monthly and tell the stakeholder the team needs more delivery time.$qb$, $qb$Ask the sponsor to tell the stakeholder that weekly reporting is excessive.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-537$qb$, $qb$A5-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A stakeholder repeatedly changes their preferred solution but says the underlying objective has not changed. The team is becoming frustrated. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Reconfirm the objective and decision criteria, compare each proposed solution against them, and agree a decision process or freeze point so preferences do not keep resetting the work.$qb$, $qb$Ask the stakeholder to choose one solution and promise not to change it again.$qb$, $qb$Continue implementing each new preference because stakeholder satisfaction is important.$qb$, $qb$Escalate the stakeholder's indecision to the sponsor.$qb$, $qb$Let the team choose the solution because they are tired of changes.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-583$qb$, $qb$A5-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A stakeholder asks for a new feature during a review, but it is unclear whether they need the feature itself or the outcome they think it will provide. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Ask what problem or outcome they are trying to achieve, clarify success criteria, and explore whether the requested feature or another solution best meets that need.$qb$, $qb$Add the feature request to the backlog and prioritize it later.$qb$, $qb$Explain why the feature may be difficult and ask whether they still want it.$qb$, $qb$Ask the product team to estimate the feature before discussing it further.$qb$, $qb$Reject the request because it was not part of the agreed scope.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-721$qb$, $qb$A5-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A stakeholder asks for a custom report every week, but after several weeks you notice they rarely open it. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Ask what decisions they expected the report to support, share the usage observation carefully, and agree whether to simplify, change cadence, or stop the report based on current need.$qb$, $qb$Stop sending it because usage shows it is unnecessary.$qb$, $qb$Keep sending it because the stakeholder requested it.$qb$, $qb$Send it monthly without asking.$qb$, $qb$Ask their manager whether the report should continue.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-951$qb$, $qb$A5-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A stakeholder asks to approve every minor design adjustment after one earlier change surprised them. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Identify the types and thresholds that need early visibility and create targeted escalation rather than copying every routine complaint.$qb$, $qb$Copy them on everything.$qb$, $qb$Refuse the request.$qb$, $qb$Send a monthly complaint list.$qb$, $qb$Ask the sponsor to decide.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-032$qb$, $qb$A5-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Your company introduces a strategic focus on sustainability. How do you align your team's routine administrative operations with this new vision?$qb$, $qb$Review daily workflows, identify practical digital-first shifts that reduce paper and resource waste, and set measurable team eco-goals.$qb$, $qb$Remind team members during project meetings to print documents double-sided whenever possible.$qb$, $qb$Wait for central management to issue specific operational guidelines on sustainability practices.$qb$, $qb$Share company sustainability announcements in team chat channels to raise general awareness.$qb$, $qb$Focus on core operational targets, assuming sustainability initiatives are managed by corporate facility teams.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-078$qb$, $qb$A5-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Your team is offered a visible initiative that senior leaders like, but it would consume capacity needed for an existing priority tied directly to the year's goals. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Compare both against the agreed objectives and value, make the capacity trade-off explicit, and align stakeholders before changing the team's commitments.$qb$, $qb$Accept the visible initiative because senior leadership is interested.$qb$, $qb$Reject it immediately because the current priority was approved first.$qb$, $qb$Ask the team to do both and reduce lower-level work.$qb$, $qb$Delay the existing priority without telling the original stakeholder.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1134$qb$, $qb$A5-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A proposed customer customization would generate good revenue but make the product harder to maintain for every future release. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Compare revenue, maintenance burden, strategic product direction and opportunity cost, and recommend whether to constrain, price, redesign or decline the customization.$qb$, $qb$Accept it because the revenue is attractive.$qb$, $qb$Reject all customizations because standardization is strategically better.$qb$, $qb$Build it now and solve maintenance later.$qb$, $qb$Ask the customer to decide whether the maintenance burden is acceptable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-124$qb$, $qb$A5-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team metric rewards fast completion, but people are now choosing easy work first while high-value complex work waits. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Show how the metric is distorting behavior, rebalance priorities around the business outcome, and recommend a measure that rewards value and timely completion rather than speed alone.$qb$, $qb$Tell the team to stop choosing easy work first.$qb$, $qb$Keep the metric and add a separate reminder about complex work.$qb$, $qb$Ask managers to assign all complex work manually.$qb$, $qb$Increase the completion target so more work gets finished.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1272$qb$, $qb$A5-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A regional team proposes a profitable local product variation that would require permanent exceptions to the company's standard operating model. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Compare local profit with ongoing complexity, scalability, customer value and strategic fit, and decide whether to standardize, price, constrain or decline the variation.$qb$, $qb$Approve it because the region knows its market.$qb$, $qb$Reject it because standardization should never be broken.$qb$, $qb$Allow it temporarily and ignore future operating cost.$qb$, $qb$Ask operations to absorb the exceptions.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1318$qb$, $qb$A5-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A business unit can extend a profitable service into a new market, but doing so would delay a capability the company has identified as essential for future growth. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Compare expansion value, strategic capability delay, resource constraints and reversibility, then make the trade-off explicitly rather than treating current profit as the only value.$qb$, $qb$Expand because the service is already profitable.$qb$, $qb$Reject expansion because future strategy should always win.$qb$, $qb$Do both and ask teams to absorb the extra workload.$qb$, $qb$Let the business unit decide without considering enterprise priorities.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1548$qb$, $qb$A5-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A local market opportunity is attractive but would require a unique pricing model that cannot be reused in the company's target markets. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Compare local profit with pricing-system complexity, support cost, strategic fit and opportunity cost before deciding whether to standardize, constrain or decline it.$qb$, $qb$Accept because the market is profitable.$qb$, $qb$Reject because unique pricing is always bad.$qb$, $qb$Launch it and let finance manage the complexity.$qb$, $qb$Let the local team decide alone.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1594$qb$, $qb$A5-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A proposed partnership would give quick access to a new customer group but require exclusivity that limits other channels for two years. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Compare access value, exclusivity cost, strategic options and exit terms, then negotiate or reject restrictions that create disproportionate future constraint.$qb$, $qb$Accept because customer access is valuable.$qb$, $qb$Reject all exclusivity clauses.$qb$, $qb$Sign and revisit the restriction later.$qb$, $qb$Let sales decide based on near-term revenue.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2422$qb$, $qb$A5-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A major customer requests a bespoke report that would consume the same analysts needed to automate a recurring report used by many teams. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Compare customer value, analyst capacity, recurring automation benefit, service commitments and alternatives before making the resource trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Respond to customer requests a bespoke report that with the standard explanation before clarifying the other person's actual need.$qb$, $qb$Add more detail about customer requests a bespoke report that even though the problem is relevance or clarity rather than missing information.$qb$, $qb$Ask another colleague to take over customer requests a bespoke report that without preserving the context already available.$qb$, $qb$Treat the concern about customer requests a bespoke report that as resistance and continue with the original message.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2847$qb$, $qb$A5-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A department can fund either a quick cost-saving project or foundational work needed for a strategic expansion next year. Resources are limited. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Compare near-term savings, strategic option value, timing, dependencies and opportunity cost, then recommend the allocation that creates stronger total value.$qb$, $qb$Choose the cost-saving project because its return is measurable.$qb$, $qb$Choose the strategic work because future priorities should always win.$qb$, $qb$Split funding equally.$qb$, $qb$Ask finance to choose the higher NPV only.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-352$qb$, $qb$A5-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A proposed cost-saving measure would improve this quarter's budget but make it harder to deliver a strategic customer experience initiative next quarter. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Assess the immediate saving against the strategic impact, quantify the trade-off, and recommend whether to modify, defer, or reject the saving based on total value.$qb$, $qb$Take the saving because current budget commitments are certain and next quarter is not.$qb$, $qb$Reject the saving because strategic initiatives should always take priority.$qb$, $qb$Implement half the saving so both objectives are partly protected.$qb$, $qb$Ask finance and the strategy team to decide between themselves.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-444$qb$, $qb$A5-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team has capacity for one additional project. One option supports an existing large customer; another builds capability needed for the company's future strategy. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Compare customer value, strategic capability, timing, risk and opportunity cost, then recommend the project that creates the stronger total value and explain what is being deferred.$qb$, $qb$Choose the existing customer because current revenue should come before future capability.$qb$, $qb$Choose the strategic capability because future strategy should override current customer needs.$qb$, $qb$Split the team between both projects even if progress is slower.$qb$, $qb$Ask senior leadership to choose because both options are important.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-628$qb$, $qb$A5-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A proposed initiative has strong short-term revenue potential but would require custom work that moves the product away from the company's chosen long-term direction. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Compare the revenue opportunity with strategic divergence, future maintenance and opportunity cost, and recommend whether to decline, constrain or structure the deal without undermining the long-term direction.$qb$, $qb$Accept the initiative because near-term revenue funds future strategy.$qb$, $qb$Reject it because any deviation from strategy is harmful.$qb$, $qb$Accept the custom work for this customer only and assume it will not affect future priorities.$qb$, $qb$Ask sales and product leaders to decide between revenue and strategy.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-812$qb$, $qb$A5-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A proposed partnership offers immediate access to customers but requires commitments that could limit future product choices. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Evaluate near-term access, strategic constraints, reversibility and opportunity cost, and negotiate or reject terms that create disproportionate long-term restriction.$qb$, $qb$Accept because customer access accelerates growth.$qb$, $qb$Reject because strategic flexibility should never be constrained.$qb$, $qb$Take the partnership for one year and assume future terms can be renegotiated.$qb$, $qb$Ask sales to decide because they own customer growth.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-046$qb$, $qb$A5-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Your team and the finance department frequently experience friction over expense approval timelines, causing delayed project vendor payments. How do you resolve this?$qb$, $qb$Schedule a joint mapping session with finance leads, identify specific workflow bottlenecks, co-create a streamlined submission SLA, and track results.$qb$, $qb$Instruct your team to submit expense requests earlier to account for finance department processing delays.$qb$, $qb$Escalate the ongoing payment delays to senior executive leadership to enforce finance compliance with vendor schedules.$qb$, $qb$Send regular daily reminder messages to finance approvers whenever vendor invoices approach due dates.$qb$, $qb$Bypass standard expense channels by requesting emergency payment overrides from your department lead for critical vendors.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-092$qb$, $qb$A5-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Your team and another function each meet their own targets, but customers experience repeated delays at the handoff between them. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Map the end-to-end flow together, agree a shared outcome and handoff measures, clarify ownership, and redesign the interface rather than optimizing local targets alone.$qb$, $qb$Ask the slower team to improve its response time.$qb$, $qb$Create a daily escalation list of delayed handoffs.$qb$, $qb$Ask senior management to choose which team owns the delay.$qb$, $qb$Keep both teams' processes unchanged because their individual targets are being met.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1194$qb$, $qb$A5-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Finance changes the timing of a billing cut-off, but customer teams continue promising the old invoice date. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Correct current customer guidance and create a reliable finance-to-customer-team change handoff with effective dates so future promises reflect the billing rule.$qb$, $qb$Ask finance to email everyone next time.$qb$, $qb$Tell customer teams to check finance policies before every promise.$qb$, $qb$Honor the old invoice date for every affected customer regardless of feasibility.$qb$, $qb$Wait for customer complaints before updating guidance.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1286$qb$, $qb$A5-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Marketing changes a promotional offer, but customer service's response templates still quote the previous terms. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Correct the current templates and create a governed marketing-to-service change handoff with effective dates and ownership before promotions go live.$qb$, $qb$Ask marketing to email customer service next time.$qb$, $qb$Tell service staff to check the marketing website before every response.$qb$, $qb$Honor the old terms for every customer who asks.$qb$, $qb$Wait for more complaints before changing the process.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1332$qb$, $qb$A5-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Sales changes a promised installation package, but the field-service checklist still assumes the old package and technicians arrive without one newly required item. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Correct the current checklist and affected jobs, then embed a sales-to-field-service change handoff with effective dates and ownership before future promises are made.$qb$, $qb$Ask sales to email technicians next time.$qb$, $qb$Tell technicians to read sales proposals before every visit.$qb$, $qb$Buy the missing item locally whenever this occurs.$qb$, $qb$Wait until enough incidents justify changing the process.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1424$qb$, $qb$A5-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Product management changes a feature name, but training material and support search terms still use the old name, causing users to think the feature disappeared. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Update current training and support references, preserve appropriate search redirects, and create a product-change handoff so naming changes propagate before release.$qb$, $qb$Ask product managers to email trainers next time.$qb$, $qb$Tell users the old and new names whenever they ask.$qb$, $qb$Keep both names in all material indefinitely.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the next scheduled training update.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1470$qb$, $qb$A5-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Finance changes a payment reference format, but customer-facing instructions still tell customers to use the old format. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Correct current customer instructions and create a finance-to-customer-team handoff so payment-reference changes are updated before they take effect.$qb$, $qb$Ask finance to email everyone next time.$qb$, $qb$Tell customer teams to check finance guidance before every message.$qb$, $qb$Accept the old reference indefinitely.$qb$, $qb$Wait for failed payments before changing the process.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1516$qb$, $qb$A5-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Compliance changes a mandatory disclaimer, but sales templates and marketing brochures continue using the previous wording. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Correct current materials and create a governed compliance-to-content change process with owners, effective dates and source control before future material is issued.$qb$, $qb$Ask compliance to email sales and marketing next time.$qb$, $qb$Tell sales and marketing to check compliance policies before every document.$qb$, $qb$Keep both disclaimers until old materials are used up.$qb$, $qb$Wait for the next scheduled template refresh.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-182$qb$, $qb$A5-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer issue passes through three teams before it is resolved because each team handles only its own part. Customers repeatedly have to repeat the same information. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Map the end-to-end journey with the three teams, identify ownership gaps and repeated handoffs, and agree a clearer route or single accountable owner for common cases.$qb$, $qb$Ask the first team to remain copied on every handoff so someone can track the case from start to finish.$qb$, $qb$Create a detailed escalation guide telling customers which team handles each type of problem.$qb$, $qb$Ask senior management to merge the teams because the current structure is causing silos.$qb$, $qb$Accept the handoffs as necessary because each team has specialized expertise.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-228$qb$, $qb$A5-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Sales promises customers a turnaround time that operations often cannot meet because the two teams plan capacity separately. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Bring sales and operations together to compare demand, capacity, and customer commitments, then agree service promises, exception rules, and a shared planning rhythm.$qb$, $qb$Ask sales to confirm every unusual promise with operations before committing it.$qb$, $qb$Ask operations to reserve extra capacity for sales requests each week.$qb$, $qb$Escalate missed promises individually whenever a customer complains.$qb$, $qb$Let sales keep setting customer expectations because operations should adapt to revenue needs.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-274$qb$, $qb$A5-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Marketing launches promotions without always informing customer support, causing sudden spikes in questions that support cannot answer. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Create a shared launch handoff with upcoming promotion details, likely customer questions, owners, and timing, and establish a regular planning touchpoint between marketing and support.$qb$, $qb$Ask marketing to email support before every major promotion.$qb$, $qb$Ask support to monitor marketing channels so they can prepare themselves.$qb$, $qb$Escalate each missed handoff to both department heads.$qb$, $qb$Increase support staffing during promotion periods without changing the handoff process.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-320$qb$, $qb$A5-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Finance and operations maintain separate forecasts for the same workload, and the numbers often differ enough to cause staffing disputes. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Bring both teams together to align definitions, assumptions, data sources, and update timing, then create a shared reconciliation process for material differences.$qb$, $qb$Ask each team to send its forecast to the other before staffing decisions.$qb$, $qb$Choose finance's forecast because budget ownership sits there.$qb$, $qb$Choose operations' forecast because they are closer to the workload.$qb$, $qb$Let both forecasts continue and ask management to decide which number to use each month.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-366$qb$, $qb$A5-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Product and support teams track different definitions of a 'known issue,' causing customers to receive inconsistent answers. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Bring the teams together to agree the definition, source of truth, ownership for updates, and how changes will reach customer-facing staff.$qb$, $qb$Ask support to use the product team's definition because product owns the feature.$qb$, $qb$Ask each team to publish its definition so staff can choose the relevant one.$qb$, $qb$Escalate inconsistent customer answers whenever they occur.$qb$, $qb$Let the definitions remain different because the teams use the term for different purposes.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-412$qb$, $qb$A5-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Two departments use separate request queues for work that often needs both teams, causing customers to submit the same request twice. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Map the shared request types, create a single intake or linked handoff with clear ownership, and ensure both teams can see the information already provided.$qb$, $qb$Tell customers which queue they should try first.$qb$, $qb$Ask each department to forward requests manually when the other team is needed.$qb$, $qb$Create a guide explaining when customers must submit to both queues.$qb$, $qb$Keep the queues separate because each department needs its own records.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-550$qb$, $qb$A5-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Operations changes a process that affects billing, but finance often learns about such changes only after invoice errors appear. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Create a cross-functional change check that identifies billing impact before operational changes go live, with finance input, ownership and communication built into the change process.$qb$, $qb$Ask operations to email finance whenever they remember a change may affect billing.$qb$, $qb$Ask finance to review operational procedures more frequently.$qb$, $qb$Add more invoice checks after process changes.$qb$, $qb$Escalate each invoice error to both department heads.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-642$qb$, $qb$A5-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Sales records customer commitments in its CRM, while delivery teams use a separate planning tool and sometimes miss special commitments. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Define which customer commitments delivery must see, create an automated or governed handoff between the systems, assign ownership for exceptions, and verify that changes remain synchronized.$qb$, $qb$Ask salespeople to email delivery whenever a commitment is unusual.$qb$, $qb$Give delivery staff access to the CRM and ask them to check it.$qb$, $qb$Copy all CRM notes manually into the planning tool each week.$qb$, $qb$Escalate missed commitments whenever they cause a customer problem.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-688$qb$, $qb$A5-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Finance changes a billing rule, but sales proposals still use the old assumption because the change is stored only in finance documentation. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Create a governed change handoff so billing-rule changes update the sales guidance or system source used in proposals, with clear ownership and effective dates.$qb$, $qb$Ask finance to email sales whenever a billing rule changes.$qb$, $qb$Ask salespeople to review finance documentation before every proposal.$qb$, $qb$Add a finance approval step to every sales proposal.$qb$, $qb$Correct proposals individually whenever the old assumption appears.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-872$qb$, $qb$A5-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Legal updates contract wording, but sales proposal templates continue using the old clause until someone notices manually. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Create a governed link between approved legal clauses and sales templates, with change ownership, version control and effective-date communication built into the update process.$qb$, $qb$Ask legal to email sales whenever wording changes.$qb$, $qb$Ask sales to check the legal folder before every proposal.$qb$, $qb$Require legal to review every proposal.$qb$, $qb$Correct templates whenever an outdated clause is discovered.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-045$qb$, $qb$A5-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$During a project post-mortem meeting, a junior team member admits to an oversight that led to a minor client deadline delay. How do you respond in the group?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge their honesty constructively, reframe the mistake as a systemic learning opportunity, and facilitate a team discussion on process safeguards.$qb$, $qb$Thank the team member for admitting the mistake and move quickly to the next meeting agenda item.$qb$, $qb$Remind the team member in front of the group of the importance of double-checking work to avoid client delays.$qb$, $qb$Schedule a private follow-up meeting with the junior staff member after the post-mortem to discuss performance impacts.$qb$, $qb$Suggest that team lead approvals be required for all future junior deliverables to prevent similar errors.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-091$qb$, $qb$A5-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$In a review meeting, a junior colleague raises a concern that contradicts the approach favored by the most senior person in the room. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Invite the evidence, thank them for raising the risk, examine the concern on its merits, and keep the discussion free from ridicule or status pressure.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to discuss the concern with you privately after the meeting.$qb$, $qb$Tell them the senior person has more context and the team should proceed.$qb$, $qb$Move quickly to the next agenda item to prevent tension.$qb$, $qb$Ask their manager why the concern was not filtered before the meeting.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1009$qb$, $qb$A5-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$During a busy shift, an experienced employee quietly admits they have never been shown how to complete a supposedly basic system step. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Respond without embarrassment or blame, arrange the needed guidance, and check whether others may share the same hidden training gap.$qb$, $qb$Tell them to read the manual after the shift.$qb$, $qb$Move the step permanently to someone else.$qb$, $qb$Ask why they did not learn it earlier.$qb$, $qb$Show them once and tell them to keep the gap private.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1193$qb$, $qb$A5-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A new employee questions a long-standing process and an experienced colleague laughs, saying, 'You'll understand after you've been here longer.' What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Stop the dismissive response, invite the question and reasoning, and evaluate whether the process still has a valid purpose rather than using tenure as the answer.$qb$, $qb$Tell the new employee to trust experienced colleagues for now.$qb$, $qb$Support the new employee's idea automatically.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to raise process questions privately in future.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the exchange because the experienced colleague may be right.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1285$qb$, $qb$A5-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A teammate raises a concern about a popular proposal, and others respond that they are being negative. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Ask for the evidence behind the concern, separate constructive risk identification from negativity, and ensure the proposal is tested against the issue before deciding.$qb$, $qb$Tell the teammate to present a solution before raising concerns.$qb$, $qb$Support the proposal because most of the team favors it.$qb$, $qb$Agree with the concern automatically to protect dissent.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to raise concerns privately next time.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1331$qb$, $qb$A5-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A teammate questions a process during a meeting, and someone replies, 'That isn't your area.' The concern could affect the whole project. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Invite the concern and evidence, evaluate its project impact regardless of functional ownership, and route follow-up to the right owner without discouraging cross-boundary voice.$qb$, $qb$Tell the teammate to raise it with the correct department later.$qb$, $qb$Agree that people should stay within their roles.$qb$, $qb$Support the concern automatically because cross-functional challenge is good.$qb$, $qb$End the discussion to protect the agenda.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1469$qb$, $qb$A5-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A junior colleague raises a credible risk and another person dismisses it because 'the senior team has already agreed.' What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Invite the junior colleague's evidence, stop the seniority-based dismissal, and test the agreed plan against the risk before closing the decision.$qb$, $qb$Tell the junior colleague the senior team has more context.$qb$, $qb$Support the risk automatically.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to raise concerns privately next time.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the exchange and continue.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1515$qb$, $qb$A5-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A teammate raises a possible customer risk but says they may be wrong and apologizes for 'wasting time.' What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Thank them for raising the signal, assess the evidence proportionately, and reinforce that uncertain but relevant risks should be surfaced rather than hidden until proven.$qb$, $qb$Tell them to investigate fully before raising concerns again.$qb$, $qb$Escalate it immediately as a confirmed risk.$qb$, $qb$Reassure them it is probably nothing.$qb$, $qb$Ask them not to raise speculative issues in meetings.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1561$qb$, $qb$A5-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A teammate raises a concern about a deadline and says they may be overreacting because everyone else seems confident. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Ask for the evidence behind the concern, assess its impact objectively, and reinforce that relevant minority concerns should be surfaced even when confidence is high elsewhere.$qb$, $qb$Tell them to trust the group unless they are certain.$qb$, $qb$Escalate the deadline immediately.$qb$, $qb$Reassure them the team probably has it covered.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to keep the concern private until more evidence appears.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-227$qb$, $qb$A5-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team member makes a reasonable suggestion, and a senior colleague dismisses it with a joke. The team member stops contributing. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Return to the suggestion on its merits, invite the team member to finish the point, and reinforce that ideas should be challenged respectfully rather than dismissed through status or ridicule.$qb$, $qb$Speak privately to the team member afterward and encourage them not to be discouraged.$qb$, $qb$Tell the senior colleague privately that the joke was unnecessary.$qb$, $qb$Move on during the meeting to avoid making the interaction more uncomfortable.$qb$, $qb$Accept the dismissal because senior colleagues are expected to challenge weaker ideas quickly.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-273$qb$, $qb$A5-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team member raises a concern that turns out to be incorrect. Another colleague laughs and says this is why people should check facts before speaking up. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Correct the factual point respectfully, thank the team member for raising the concern, and make clear that good-faith questions are welcome even when they prove unfounded.$qb$, $qb$Correct the fact and move on quickly so the team member is not embarrassed further.$qb$, $qb$Tell the colleague who laughed privately afterward that the comment was unhelpful.$qb$, $qb$Advise the team member to verify concerns more carefully before raising them next time.$qb$, $qb$Say nothing because the concern was incorrect and the team needs higher-quality input.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2757$qb$, $qb$A5-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A common shortcut skips required customer-consent evidence; a senior colleague says customers rarely object. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Assess the consent requirement directly and stop or redesign the shortcut if it bypasses required evidence.$qb$, $qb$Implement a practical interim adjustment to address immediate operational needs while monitoring results.$qb$, $qb$Maintain existing procedures temporarily until formal company-wide guidance is published.$qb$, $qb$Escalate the issue to departmental leadership without first gathering supporting data or options.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the operational discrepancy and proceed with business as usual.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2838$qb$, $qb$A5-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A junior teammate raises a concern, and another colleague responds, 'We've done this for years; don't overthink it.' What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Invite the concern and evidence, evaluate whether it reveals a real risk, and reinforce that established practice can still be questioned respectfully.$qb$, $qb$Agree with the experienced colleague because history is useful evidence.$qb$, $qb$Tell the junior teammate to investigate privately.$qb$, $qb$Support the junior concern automatically to encourage speaking up.$qb$, $qb$Move on because the discussion is slowing the meeting.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-319$qb$, $qb$A5-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague challenges your idea in a meeting and later apologizes for disagreeing with you in front of others. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Tell them respectful challenge was appropriate, discuss the substance of their concern openly, and reinforce that disagreement does not require an apology.$qb$, $qb$Accept the apology and reassure them that you are not offended.$qb$, $qb$Tell them to raise disagreements privately next time to avoid awkward meetings.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the apology because responding may make the situation more uncomfortable.$qb$, $qb$Remind them that public disagreement should be used only for serious issues.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-365$qb$, $qb$A5-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A teammate admits they do not understand a topic that everyone else appears comfortable with. Another person sighs and says the team has already covered it twice. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Respond to the question respectfully, clarify the topic or arrange follow-up, and reinforce that asking for clarification is preferable to silently proceeding with a misunderstanding.$qb$, $qb$Answer the question quickly and ignore the other person's reaction.$qb$, $qb$Tell the teammate to review the earlier material before asking again.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to speak with you privately after the meeting.$qb$, $qb$Move on because repeating material wastes the rest of the team's time.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-411$qb$, $qb$A5-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A junior employee raises a concern that slows a meeting, and another participant says, 'We don't have time for basic questions.' What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Protect the right to raise the concern, determine whether it affects the decision, answer or assign it efficiently, and reinforce that relevant questions are welcome regardless of seniority.$qb$, $qb$Answer the question quickly and move on without addressing the comment.$qb$, $qb$Tell the junior employee to bring basic questions to you privately next time.$qb$, $qb$Agree that time is short and postpone the question until after the decision.$qb$, $qb$Let the participant's comment stand because meetings need to remain efficient.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-503$qb$, $qb$A5-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague raises an unpopular concern in a meeting. The concern is relevant, but several people immediately dismiss it because it may delay the project. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Ensure the concern is heard and assessed against evidence and impact, then decide proportionately whether it requires action without allowing schedule pressure to silence it.$qb$, $qb$Thank the colleague and move on because the concern can be reviewed later.$qb$, $qb$Ask the colleague to prove the concern before the group spends more time on it.$qb$, $qb$Support the majority because delaying the project for one concern is unfair.$qb$, $qb$Ask the colleague to raise such concerns privately in future.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-549$qb$, $qb$A5-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team member admits in a review that they made an error others had not noticed. The error is recoverable. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Thank them for surfacing it, focus first on recovery and learning, and reinforce that early disclosure is expected while still addressing any preventable cause.$qb$, $qb$Tell them the honesty is appreciated but issue a warning so others understand errors have consequences.$qb$, $qb$Correct the error quietly so the employee is not embarrassed.$qb$, $qb$Ask why they did not prevent the error before praising their honesty.$qb$, $qb$Say nothing about the disclosure and focus only on fixing the work.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-641$qb$, $qb$A5-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team member points out a risk in your own proposal during a meeting. You disagree with part of their reasoning, and everyone is watching your response. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Thank them for raising it, examine the risk openly, challenge the reasoning respectfully where needed, and show that disagreement with your proposal is evaluated on evidence rather than status.$qb$, $qb$Explain immediately why their reasoning is wrong so the team does not become confused.$qb$, $qb$Thank them and move the risk discussion to a private conversation.$qb$, $qb$Accept their concern publicly even though you think it is weak, to encourage speaking up.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the concern because debating your own proposal may undermine confidence.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-687$qb$, $qb$A5-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A teammate proposes an unconventional solution and several people laugh before hearing the explanation. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Stop the dismissive reaction, invite the teammate to explain the reasoning, and evaluate the proposal against the same criteria as other ideas before deciding.$qb$, $qb$Ask the teammate to explain the idea privately after the meeting.$qb$, $qb$Tell the group not to laugh but move to the next agenda item.$qb$, $qb$Support the unconventional idea so the teammate feels encouraged.$qb$, $qb$Let the reaction pass because teams need freedom to respond naturally.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
